99	/**
100	 * How many jobs `main()`'s loop will run concurrently. Was hardcoded to 1
101	 * (fully sequential — a single slow BACKUP_CREATE could leave unrelated
102	 * PROVISION jobs QUEUED for 20+ hours, confirmed against production timing
103	 * data); this is the platform-wide cap on that.
104	 *
105	 * Deliberately modest, not unlimited, and NOT sized off Node's own CPU/event
106	 * loop — almost every handler in `handlers` below is I/O-bound (a Proxmox API
107	 * call + `waitForTask`, an SSH exec, a guest-agent poll), so the worker
108	 * process itself is rarely the bottleneck. What actually caps this:
109	 *   - Proxmox's pveproxy API is a single process per node with its own
110	 *     request/worker limits, and PROVISION's clone + BACKUP_CREATE's vzdump
111	 *     both saturate that node's storage I/O while they run — piling many of
112	 *     those onto the SAME node at once slows every one of them down rather
113	 *     than truly parallelizing (the fleet has 5 nodes, but a burst of orders
114	 *     is not guaranteed to spread evenly across them).
115	 *   - Each job that touches a guest opens its own SSH connection (and often
116	 *     `sshpass`/child processes) via lib/ssh-exec.ts; too many concurrent
117	 *     connections is connection-overhead, not throughput.
118	 *   - The worker host's own CPU/RAM (see MEMORY.md's node-RAM-tuning /
119	 *     disk-full-outage incidents — this primary node has hit real resource
120	 *     limits before) still needs headroom for the Next.js app running
121	 *     alongside it.
122	 * 8 is a conservative middle ground: enough to turn a 20-order burst from
123	 * ~80 minutes of pure queue wait down to a few sequential "waves" of 8, while
124	 * staying well short of anything likely to rate-limit a single Proxmox node
125	 * or exhaust this host's own resources. Override with WORKER_CONCURRENCY if
126	 * real production behavior says otherwise.
